<div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<div class="note"><b class="h">Local claim risks in Oakland</b><ul class="chips"><li>Hillside wildfire</li><li>Earthquake &amp; fire following</li><li>Storm leaks</li><li>Falling trees</li><li>Water losses</li></ul><p class="hoods"><b>Neighborhoods we often work in:</b> Rockridge, Montclair, Temescal, Grand Lake, Oakland Hills, Fruitvale</p></div>

<h2>The 1991 Oakland Hills fire</h2>
<p>In October 1991, the Oakland Hills fire, also called the Tunnel Fire, destroyed thousands of homes in the hills above Rockridge and Montclair in a single day, driven by hot, dry Diablo winds. It remains one of the costliest urban wildfires in California history and still shapes building standards and insurance in the hills. An insurance appraiser in Oakland has to understand both hillside rebuild costs and how fire-resistant construction requirements affect them.</p>
<h2>Earthquakes</h2>
<p>The Hayward Fault runs through Oakland and is considered one of the most hazardous faults in the country. The 1989 Loma Prieta earthquake caused the collapse of the Cypress Street Viaduct in West Oakland. Earthquake damage requires separate coverage, but fire following an earthquake is generally covered under standard homeowner policies.</p>
<h2>Neighborhoods and housing</h2>
<p>Rockridge, Temescal and Grand Lake have Craftsman bungalows and brown-shingle homes from the early 1900s, with old-growth redwood trim and plaster walls. The hills have mid-century and newer custom homes on steep lots. Fruitvale and the flatlands have Victorians and older apartment buildings. Matching original materials and accounting for hillside access are common sources of disagreement.</p>
<h2>Storms and trees</h2>
<p>The winter of 2022–23 brought down large trees across the Oakland Hills, damaging homes and blocking roads. Tree impacts, roof leaks and wind-driven rain are among the most common storm claims in the city.</p>

<h2>Services available in Oakland</h2>
<p>Every California Insurance Appraiser service is available to property owners in Oakland and the rest of Alameda County, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/disputed-claim-services/policyholder-appraiser-representation/">Appraisal Clause Representation</a></li><li><a href="/disputed-claim-services/home-claim-appraisal/">Homeowner Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/disputed-claim-services/commercial-hoa-claim-appraisal/">Commercial &amp; HOA Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/disputed-claim-services/roof-damage-appraisal/">Roof Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/disputed-claim-services/santa-ana-wind-damage-appraisal/">Wind Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/disputed-claim-services/atmospheric-river-storm-claim-appraisal/">Atmospheric River &amp; Storm Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/disputed-claim-services/water-leak-claim-appraisal/">Water Leak Claim Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Questions from Oakland property owners</h2><div class="qa"><details><summary>Are hillside rebuild costs higher in Oakland?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Usually. Steep lots, retaining walls and limited access add cost, and we price them specifically.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you serve Berkeley and the East Bay?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Yes, including Berkeley, Alameda, San Leandro, Hayward, Piedmont and Walnut Creek.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is earthquake damage covered?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Only with separate earthquake coverage. Fire following an earthquake is generally covered under a standard policy.</p></div></details></div>
